+## 0. Proof principles
+
+Evidence must try to disprove a claim. A capture is not proof until a separately motivated evaluator judges it. Every claim cites an observed mechanism; an unreachable flow is a defect; and no validation report may raise readiness without meeting its named gate.
+
+## 1. Target Launch Platforms
+
+| Platform | Build artifact | Distribution |
+|----------|----------------|--------------|
+| Web application (inferred from the B2B SaaS archetype; revise if implementation selects another target) | Clean production web bundle served locally in production mode | Hosted web service |
+
+## 2. Valid Proof Sources
+
+### Web application
+
+- A clean production bundle, not a development server, with recorded facilitator LARP steps, screenshots, accessibility tree, browser console, and network trace.
+- A real confirmation attempt whose persisted action includes the confirmed text, owner, due date, workspace, idempotency key, and delivery state; read back against a pre-attempt baseline.
+- A live read as an allowed workspace principal and a separate denied workspace principal against the real authorization layer.
+- A real delivery-provider attempt to a controlled test recipient, or a provider-confirmed terminal failure, followed by a read-back of the persisted outcome and attempt identifier.
+- A concurrency probe that submits the same confirmed action twice and proves one delivery attempt or an explicit duplicate rejection.
+
+### Promise discharge map
+
+| Claim | Claim type | Admissible evidence | Failure-path probe |
+|-------|------------|---------------------|--------------------|
+| PRM-001: no reminder precedes explicit confirmation of text, owner, and due date | acceptance | `live-probe` of the real create/confirm boundary plus `db-catalog` baseline/read-back | Submit each missing field and a bypassed/unconfirmed request; require rejection, no delivery attempt, and no scheduled record. |
+| PRM-002: a workspace cannot read another workspace's transcript, action, or delivery status | visibility | `live-probe` reads as both real workspace principals, plus `db-catalog` read-back | Create all three record types in workspace A; as workspace B attempt direct and list reads. Any returned row, count, or status is failure. |
+| PRM-003: a confirmed reminder is delivered exactly once or exposes a truthful terminal failure state | persistence | `live-probe` delivery attempt plus baselined `db-catalog` read-back | Repeat the same confirmation concurrently and replay after a timeout; then force the transport to reject. Fail if attempts exceed one without explicit duplicate handling, or if UI/state reports delivery without an observed outcome. |
+
+### 2b. Typed citations and machine admissibility
+
+A typed citation is `<citation-type>:<path>[@<sha>]`. The type states which instrument observed the claim; an untyped path is surrogate proof until it is typed and checked.
+
+| Token | Instrument |
+|-------|------------|
+| `test` | Unit or integration suite in the project harness. |
+| `mutation-guard` | Guard removal observed to make the protected test fail. |
+| `live-probe` | Request/read/write at the deployed target or real engine as a named principal. |
+| `db-catalog` | Live datastore introspection or baselined datastore read-back. |
+| `ax-dump` | Accessibility tree from the baked client. |
+| `geometry` | Measured running-screen geometry. |
+| `capture` | Screenshot, recording, or transcript. |
+| `device-walk` | Human attestation on the baked artifact. |
+| `model-eval` | Control-versus-treatment result on the shipped model. |
+| `static` | Source, lint, type, or grep assertion. |
+
+| Claim type | Admissible citation types |
+|------------|---------------------------|
+| `correctness` | `test` `mutation-guard` `live-probe` `db-catalog` `static` `ax-dump` `geometry` `capture` `device-walk` `model-eval` |
+| `visibility` | `live-probe` `db-catalog` `device-walk` |
+| `acceptance` | `live-probe` `db-catalog` `device-walk` |
+| `persistence` | `live-probe` `db-catalog` |
+| `fit` | `ax-dump` `geometry` `device-walk` |
+| `behaviour` | `model-eval` `device-walk` |
+
+## 3. Invalid Proof Sources
+
+- Development-server, Storybook-only, or localhost screenshots for UX-RC, SHIP-RC, or SHIP claims.
+- Mocked authentication or delivery transport for PRM-002 or PRM-003 claims.
+- A privileged database/service client used to prove workspace isolation.
+- A transport request log without a persisted pre/post baseline and terminal outcome read-back.
+- A UI success state, timer, or optimistic notification cited as delivery evidence.
+- A single-user LARP cited as proof of multi-workspace isolation.
+- A green test suite or static scan cited for what a real principal can see or what a real transport accepted.
+- Unadjudicated captures, tautological tests, silent skips, altered test harnesses, graders, or CI in the same graded change.
+
+## 4. Required Runtime LARP / Integration Stress Tests
+
+### UX-RC LARPs
+
+| Persona | Flow | Evidence required |
+|---------|------|-------------------|
+| Team facilitator | Draft action → enter text, owner, due date → explicit confirmation → observe scheduled state | Built-bundle recording, AX tree, console/network trace, persisted record read-back. |
+| Facilitator with invalid input | Attempt confirmation with each required field absent or malformed | Recording plus boundary response; no record and no delivery attempt. |
+| Workspace B facilitator | Sign in after workspace A creates transcript/action/status data → attempt list and direct reads | Principal-labelled live-read logs for both allow and deny directions. |
+| Delivery-failure facilitator | Confirm action while controlled transport rejects/times out → return to status | Recording and persisted terminal state that names failure/retry truthfully, never “delivered.” |
+
+For every LARP, DOES-IT-WORK and IS-IT-GOOD are separate judgments. A quality pass requires per-screen critique; no runtime evidence exists yet, so the current state remains NO-SHIP.
+
+## 5. Quality Judgment & Scoring Protocol
+
+Functional, on-contract, felt-good, and taste are independent axes. A score above 8 requires a distinct skeptical note and no active P0/P1; an active P0/P1 caps the affected axis at 4. Current evidence is absent, so no score is claimed.
+
